13:33:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're pointing to a real and important observation: humans are incredibly adaptable, and many people *do* seem to function well on radically different diets. This can make it seem like the metabolic path doesn't matter much—or that the body can handle anything as long as calories and basic nutrients are present. But let's dig deeper into why this apparent contradiction exists. ### 1. "Functioning just fine" is a low bar When we say someone is "functioning just fine," we usually mean they're not bedridden, they can work, they have reasonable energy, and they haven't developed an obvious acute illness. That's a very different standard from *optimal long-term health*. Consider this analogy: Many people drive cars with misaligned wheels, low tire pressure, or dirty oil. The car still moves forward. It functions "just fine" for years—until the tires wear out prematurely, the engine seizes, or the alignment destroys the suspension. The driver may not notice the gradual degradation until a major failure occurs. Similarly, a vegan who eats mostly junk food (vegan cookies, fries, and soda) might feel okay in their 20s but develop nutrient deficiencies or metabolic issues in their 40s. A carnivore who eats only fatty meat might feel energetic for a year but eventually show signs of elevated LDL cholesterol, gut dysbiosis, or increased inflammation (depending on genetics). The point is that **many people "function" despite suboptimal pathways, but the cost accumulates slowly.** ### 2. Individual variation masks universal consequences Not everyone responds the same way to the same diet because of differences in: - **Genetics** (e.g., insulin sensitivity, fat metabolism, cholesterol clearance) - **Gut microbiome composition** (can change how fiber, fat, or protein are processed) - **Epigenetics and metabolic history** (prior dieting, exercise, toxin exposure) - **Hormonal status** (thyroid, sex hormones, cortisol) - **Activity level** (muscle mass, exercise type, and frequency) So while one person might thrive on a high-carb, low-fat vegan diet, another might develop blood sugar spikes and fatigue. Similarly, one person might feel great on a carnivore diet while another gets constipation, electrolyte imbalances, or high cholesterol. The *average* outcome across a population may show trends, but individuals can be outliers due to these variables. ### 3. The body has powerful compensatory mechanisms Your body doesn't passively accept whatever you eat; it actively tries to maintain homeostasis. For example: - **On a low-carb diet:** Your liver ramps up gluconeogenesis to make glucose for the brain, and your muscles become better at burning fat. - **On a high-carb diet:** Your pancreas produces more insulin, and your cells become more sensitive to it (unless overfed). - **On a low-protein diet:** Your kidneys conserve nitrogen, and your body becomes more efficient at recycling amino acids. These adaptations allow people to survive on very different macronutrient ratios. But every adaptation has a cost—like a car running on a different octane fuel; it may run, but with different efficiency, wear, and emissions. Over decades, those costs can compound. ### 4. Survivorship bias and visible failures You see people who *succeed* on various diets because those who fail often drop out or change diets. For every thriving long-term vegan, there are many who gave up due to fatigue, bloating, or nutritional deficiencies. For every carnivore advocate, there are those who experienced gut issues or heart palpitations. The internet amplifies success stories and minimizes silent failures. Also, many people who appear to function fine on extreme diets may have underlying issues that aren't visible—like elevated inflammation markers, insulin resistance, low bone density, or subclinical nutrient deficiencies. They don't *feel* sick, but their body is paying a hidden price. ### 5. The shared commonalities across successful diets If you look at people who genuinely thrive on very different diets, they almost always share core principles: - **They avoid ultra-processed foods** (high in refined sugar, seed oils, additives). - **They eat adequate protein and essential nutrients.** - **They eat enough fiber (from plants or from animal sources like collagen/glycine)** to support gut health. - **They don't overeat calorie-dense foods chronically.** - **They incorporate movement, sleep, and stress management.** These common factors are more important than the macronutrient split. A well-planned vegan diet can be healthy; a well-planned carnivore diet can also be healthy. But a poorly planned version of either (e.g., vegan junk food, or carnivore with only lean meat and no organ meats) will fail. ### The bottom line You're right: people *can* function on very different diets, and the fact that they do might seem to contradict the idea that the metabolic path is critical. But think of it this way: - **The path matters, but the human body is a resilient machine.** It can tolerate a wide range of inputs for a while. - **The consequences of a poor path are often delayed and subtle**—not instant, not obvious. - **Individual variation means some people get lucky** (genetically or microbiomically) and can handle a suboptimal path without obvious harm. - **The real question isn't "can you survive on it?" but "will it help you thrive for 80 years?"** So while you can observe many people "functioning fine" on different diets, the metabolic pathways still exist. The fact that some people avoid the negative outcomes doesn't mean the pathways are irrelevant—it means those people are either highly adapted, compensating well, or the damage hasn't accumulated enough to be visible yet. For most people, a balanced, whole-food diet that matches their individual needs will outperform any extreme approach over the long haul.
