athways when food is scarce. This flexibility means we *can* survive on: - A diet of mostly white rice and beans (traditional in some cultures) - A diet of mostly meat and fat (traditional Arctic diets) - A diet of mostly processed carbs and oils (modern Western diet) But survival is not the same as thriving. The traditional diets that allowed long-term health were whole-food-based, nutrient-dense, and matched to the environment. The modern processed diet leads to chronic disease over decades—but in the short term, people "function fine." ### 4. If diet truly didn't matter, why do chronic diseases exist? If all diets were equal, we wouldn't see: - Epidemics of obesity, type 2 diabetes, and heart disease in populations eating processed Western diets. - Dramatic improvements in health when people switch to whole-food diets (lower blood pressure, better cholesterol, weight loss, more energy). - Differences in longevity between populations (e.g., Okinawans eating a plant-heavy diet vs. Westerners eating a processed diet). The fact that many individuals *seem* fine on poor diets doesn't mean the diet is harmless—it means the disease process is slow and hidden. It's like saying "I know smokers who lived to 90, so smoking must not matter." The exceptions don't disprove the rule; they just show that other factors (genetics, luck, lifestyle) can offset some of the damage. ### 5. The real test is not "do they feel okay?" but "how healthy are they actually?" How many of the people you know on various diets have had comprehensive blood work? How many know their: - Fasting insulin or HOMA-IR (insulin resistance) - HbA1c (average blood sugar) - Homocysteine (B-vitamin status) - hs-CRP (inflammation) - Vitamin D, B12, iron levels - Liver enzymes (fatty liver) Most people don't check these. They rely on how they feel, which is a poor metric. Your friend on vegan junk food might have perfectly normal vitamin levels—or he might be borderline deficient without knowing it. ### In short: Your observation is real, but the conclusion is wrong. You see people on different diets all "doing fine." That's true. But that doesn't mean diet doesn't matter. It means: - **Humans are resilient**—we can tolerate a wide range of inputs for a long time. - **The effects of poor diet are slow and silent**—they don't show up as immediate "failure." - **You are seeing survivors**—the ones who could handle the diet, not the ones who couldn't. If you want to know whether diet matters, don't look at how people feel today. Look at the long-term population data, the disease trends, and the blood markers. That's where the truth shows up.
