me people have genetic variants that: - Give them very efficient cholesterol clearance (they can eat saturated fat without raising LDL). - Provide high antioxidant capacity (neutralizing oxidative stress from processed foods). - Allow them to maintain insulin sensitivity despite high sugar intake. These are genuine outliers—perhaps 1 in 1000 or 1 in 10,000. They are exceptions that prove the rule: most people cannot do the same. ### 4. The people you're describing are the statistical 1% If you took 1000 people and fed them a diet of burgers and fries for life: - Fewer than 1% would run marathons and have perfect blood work at age 60. - The other 99% would show elevated risk factors, weight gain, or disease. You happen to know (or know of) the 1%. That's survivorship bias—you're not seeing the thousands who didn't fare as well. ### 5. The real test: compare them to a well-fed runner Imagine two marathon runners: - **Runner A:** Lives on burgers and fries, but has excellent genetics, trains hard, and has "perfect" basic blood work. - **Runner B:** Eats a nutrient-dense whole-foods diet, trains similarly, also has excellent genetics. If you compared their *advanced* markers—coronary artery calcium score, carotid intima-media thickness, cognitive testing, mitochondrial function—Runner B would almost certainly have a better profile. The damage from the poor diet is subtle, but it's there. ### In short Your observation is real: *some* people can eat poorly, exercise heavily, and appear perfectly healthy on basic tests. But they are the outliers. Their existence does not mean the diet is safe for everyone—or even for them in the long run, if more sensitive testing were done. The vast majority of people who eat that way will *not* have perfect blood work, and will experience health decline over time. You're right to notice them. But they are the exception, not the rule.
