e debt is accumulating. Eventually, the bill comes due. ### A thought experiment Imagine two identical twins. One eats a junk food diet, the other eats a whole-foods diet. Both exercise the same amount, have the same stress levels, and the same genetics. Do you honestly believe that after 20 years, they would have identical: - Arterial health - Liver fat - Insulin sensitivity - Inflammatory markers - Cognitive function - Longevity If you think the junk food twin would be worse off, then you agree that diet matters—even if the difference isn't visible in daily life for a long time. ### In short You've seen people who appear to thrive on poor diets. That's a real observation. But it doesn't prove diet is irrelevant. It proves that: - The human body is resilient. - Some people are genetically lucky. - Exercise and lifestyle can compensate for a while. - Many effects of diet are delayed and invisible. The existence of outliers doesn't invalidate the rule. It just means the rule has exceptions. For most people, most of the time, what you eat profoundly shapes how you age, how you feel, and how long you live. Your friends might be the exceptions—but are you willing to bet your own health on being one?
